# Coding

These are non-negotiable mental laws for ShopStack Portal. They exist to prevent fragile UI, prevent over-engineering, keep velocity high as the app grows, make code easy to delete, and make the UI impossible to crash. If a decision is unclear, **default to these rules**.

## 0. The prime directive

**Code must be easier to DELETE than to WRITE.**

If removing a feature requires touching many files, understanding hidden behavior, or breaks unrelated screens — the design is already wrong.

## 1. UI is not allowed to think

UI renders. UI does not decide.

UI **may** do: layout, rendering, styling, trivial presentation branching.
UI **may not** do: business logic, side effects, API calls, retries, data fetching, non-trivial derivation.

```tsx
// ❌ WRONG
if (user.isPremium) {
  await upgradeUser()
}

// ✅ CORRECT
<Button disabled={!vm.canUpgrade} onClick={actions.upgrade} />
```

If a component contains a behavior-changing `if/else`, it is not UI.

## 2. Data flows in one direction only

```
API → Normalizer → Store/Query → Screen Hook → Page → Component
```

Never reverse. (See `data-flow.md` for the full version.)

## 3. All external data is hostile

If it can be null, it **will** be null. APIs lie. Backends change. Fields disappear.

Normalize **once** at the boundary (in `*.normalizers.ts`, with zod). After normalization, downstream code does not need defensive checks.

## 4. Null-safety

Optional chaining `?.` is a *last line of defense*, not a strategy.

```tsx
// ❌ WRONG — leaks backend instability into UI
<Text>{user?.name}</Text>

// ✅ CORRECT — normalize early
const user = UserSchema.parse(raw) // zod fills defaults, throws on missing
<Text>{user.name}</Text>
```

`?.` is **allowed** only for truly optional relationships where absence is a valid domain state (`user.avatarUrl && <Avatar src={user.avatarUrl} />`, `settings?.theme`).
`?.` is **forbidden** for conceptually-required fields (`user?.name`, `order?.id`).

## 5. Arrays are never optional

A list is **always** an array.

```ts
// ❌ WRONG
items?: Item[]

// ✅ CORRECT
items: Item[]   // missing → [], failed → [], null response → []
```

If `.map()` can crash, the design is wrong.

## 6. Stores are boring on purpose

Zustand stores hold state and expose setters. **That's it.** No async, no API calls, no derivation, no business rules. (See `zustand-stores.md`.)

## 7. Side effects have hard boundaries

Async logic must be **contained** or it spreads.

- **Allowed:** API modules, screen hooks, TanStack Query queries/mutations
- **Forbidden:** components, pages, Zustand stores

## 8. Every screen has exactly one logic hook

Hooks own behavior. Pages own layout. One screen → one `use<Name>Screen.ts`.

## 9. Every async screen has exactly four states

`loading | error | empty | success`. No exceptions, no shortcuts.

```ts
type AsyncStatus = "loading" | "error" | "empty" | "success"
```

## 10. Screen hook is the brain

The screen hook returns exactly three things:

- `status: AsyncStatus`
- `vm` — derived, UI-ready data (the view-model contract)
- `actions` — stable callbacks

## 11. View model (vm) — the UI contract

`vm` is the **only** data shape the UI reasons about. It contains derived, UI-ready data. **No functions, no raw API objects, no side effects.** Keep it small and intentional.

```ts
// ❌ WRONG — leaks every intermediate state
vm: { users, rawUsers, filteredUsers, sortedUsers, hasUsers, isEmpty }

// ✅ CORRECT
vm: { users, canRefresh }
```

## 12. Memoization is about stability, not micro-optimization

- `vm` **must** be `useMemo`'d
- `actions` **must** be `useCallback`'d
- **Do not** memoize trivial values (`users.length === 0`)

Memoize only when (a) values are passed to memoized children, (b) values are non-trivially derived, or (c) identity stability is required for an effect dependency.

## 13. Pages do not fetch data

Pages branch on `status` and render. Nothing else.

## 14. Components are pure and dumb

Components render props. They do not fetch, do not derive meaning, do not mutate state. They may have local UI state (open/closed, hover) but never domain state.

## 15. Fail fast in dev, fail safe in UI

Developers see crashes. Users see messages.

- API layer throws real errors with context
- Screen hooks convert errors to user-safe strings via `toUserMessage(e)`
- UI never throws

## 16. Duplication > wrong abstraction

A bad abstraction is harder to delete than three duplicates. Wait for the third occurrence before extracting.

## 17. Naming is a design decision

If a name is hard, the design is wrong. (See `naming.md`.)

## 18. Optimize for reading, not writing

Explicit > clever. Predictable > elegant. A junior should be able to read a screen pair top-to-bottom and understand it.

## 19. No dead code

Delete commented-out code. Delete unused exports. Delete `TODO: remove this` from six months ago. `git` is the archive.

## 20. The exit rule

Before merging:

> "Can another developer understand this screen by reading only the hook and page in under 30 seconds?"

If not — simplify, split, rename, delete.

## Lint, format, build are sacred

After **any** change:

```bash
npm install     # if deps changed
npm run lint    # --max-warnings 0
npm run build   # tsc -b + vite build
npm test        # vitest
```

Zero warnings. Zero failures. No `--no-verify`.

## Final rule

**The best code is code that does not exist.** Delete aggressively. Normalize early. Memoize intentionally. Protect the UI at all costs.
